00:06:12.310 --> 00:06:13.990
but we have guides
for that, as well.

00:06:13.990 --> 00:06:14.530
MICHELLE CASBON: Wonderful.

00:06:14.530 --> 00:06:16.090
MARK MANDEL: Yeah,
it's not too bad.

00:06:16.090 --> 00:06:17.110
Awesome.

00:06:17.110 --> 00:06:18.700
So why don't we go
chat with Viktor

00:06:18.700 --> 00:06:19.910
about Confluent and Kafka?

00:06:19.910 --> 00:06:21.160
MICHELLE CASBON: Sounds great.

00:06:21.160 --> 00:06:22.201
MARK MANDEL: Let's do it.

00:06:22.201 --> 00:06:25.430
Thanks so much to Viktor Gamov
for coming to join us today,

00:06:25.430 --> 00:06:27.650
developer advocate
for Confluent.

00:06:27.650 --> 00:06:28.810
Viktor, how are you doing?

00:06:28.810 --> 00:06:29.768
VIKTOR GAMOV: Hi, guys.

00:06:29.768 --> 00:06:31.240
Very excited to be here.

00:06:31.240 --> 00:06:33.070
Longtime listener,
first time caller.

00:06:33.070 --> 00:06:35.611
MARK MANDEL: [LAUGHS] I think,
actually, you're the first one

00:06:35.611 --> 00:06:37.010
to make that joke.

00:06:37.010 --> 00:06:37.510
Excellent.

00:06:37.510 --> 00:06:39.010
Well, thanks so
much for coming down

00:06:39.010 --> 00:06:40.955
to talk to us about
Confluent and Kafka.

00:06:40.955 --> 00:06:43.330
Before we get stuck into that,
tell us about who you are,

00:06:43.330 --> 00:06:44.290
and what do you do?

00:06:44.290 --> 00:06:46.510
VIKTOR GAMOV: I'm a developer
advocate at Confluent.

00:06:46.510 --> 00:06:50.680
What I do, I like to talk
to developers, engineers,

00:06:50.680 --> 00:06:53.680
administrators, architects,
about Kafka and streaming

00:06:53.680 --> 00:06:54.670
platform.

00:06:54.670 --> 00:06:59.055
It's very short, but we
can dive deeper if needed.

00:06:59.055 --> 00:07:01.180
MICHELLE CASBON: So can
you tell us, what is Kafka?

00:07:01.180 --> 00:07:02.360
VIKTOR GAMOV: Prepared
question, right?

00:07:02.360 --> 00:07:02.890
MICHELLE CASBON: No, not at all.

00:07:02.890 --> 00:07:04.350
None of this ever is prepared.

00:07:04.350 --> 00:07:05.770
VIKTOR GAMOV: [LAUGHS] Yeah.

00:07:05.770 --> 00:07:08.800
Essentially, Kafka is what
we call streaming platform.

00:07:08.800 --> 00:07:11.645
And interesting enough that,
when you talk to people,

00:07:11.645 --> 00:07:13.260
it depends on who
you're talking to.

00:07:13.260 --> 00:07:15.760
If you're talking to people who
have a messaging background,

00:07:15.760 --> 00:07:18.880
they can feel that, oh, it looks
like new messaging solution.

00:07:18.880 --> 00:07:21.880
If you talk to people who
have a databases background,

00:07:21.880 --> 00:07:24.460
they might say, oh, it looks
like distributed database,

00:07:24.460 --> 00:07:27.190
because of the nature of the
data that we're putting there.

00:07:27.190 --> 00:07:30.610
So Kafka is a very
unique beast in the world

00:07:30.610 --> 00:07:31.840
of distributive systems.

00:07:31.840 --> 00:07:34.390
But essentially what we call
it is a streaming platform

00:07:34.390 --> 00:07:39.170
that allows you to not only
consume results in real time,

00:07:39.170 --> 00:07:40.510
but also do processing.

00:07:40.510 --> 00:07:44.470
Because usually Kafka
is Apache project,

00:07:44.470 --> 00:07:45.730
includes multiple things--

00:07:45.730 --> 00:07:47.800
that brokers itself,
stream processing library

00:07:47.800 --> 00:07:49.690
called Kafka Streams,
and the library

00:07:49.690 --> 00:07:52.415
that allows to easy
integration with data sources

00:07:52.415 --> 00:07:53.290
called Kafka Connect.

00:07:53.290 --> 00:07:56.140
So this is a very short
version, but again, we

00:07:56.140 --> 00:07:58.450
can go as deep as
you want, and as deep

00:07:58.450 --> 00:07:59.890
as your listeners want to.

00:07:59.890 --> 00:08:00.250
MARK MANDEL: Absolutely.

00:08:00.250 --> 00:08:02.770
So what sort of things do
people tend to use Kafka for?

00:08:02.770 --> 00:08:04.660
I mean, you mentioned that some
people think of it in one way,

00:08:04.660 --> 00:08:05.800
and other people think
of it in another.

00:08:05.800 --> 00:08:07.410
What are some typical use cases?

00:08:07.410 --> 00:08:09.070
VIKTOR GAMOV: When the
Kafka was developed back

00:08:09.070 --> 00:08:10.653
in the day on the
LinkedIn, they tried

00:08:10.653 --> 00:08:13.720
to solve one of the
problems to increase speed

00:08:13.720 --> 00:08:17.140
of data ingestion
into Hadoop, and they

00:08:17.140 --> 00:08:20.800
started to move from the
batch-oriented pipelines, where

00:08:20.800 --> 00:08:23.380
they need to wait until
all data will arrive

00:08:23.380 --> 00:08:25.900
into some like LinkedIn
area where they can start

00:08:25.900 --> 00:08:28.930
processing, to the more
like, OK, do we really

00:08:28.930 --> 00:08:30.550
need to wait for all this data?

00:08:30.550 --> 00:08:34.610
Because our algorithms not
necessarily need to have in one

00:08:34.610 --> 00:08:35.110
place.

00:08:35.110 --> 00:08:38.840
So we can do this and process
this data as it comes.

00:08:38.840 --> 00:08:43.539
So this is where idea of stream
processing, and essentially

00:08:43.539 --> 00:08:47.230
infinite stream of data that
comes into the system, where

00:08:47.230 --> 00:08:50.200
we can process this
data as it comes instead

00:08:50.200 --> 00:08:52.450
of having this buffer,
or after that we can

00:08:52.450 --> 00:08:54.050
process at the end of the day.

00:08:54.050 --> 00:08:56.370
So this is one of
the use case, where

00:08:56.370 --> 00:09:00.160
the people start using this
in a very limited capacity.

00:09:00.160 --> 00:09:03.160
They started, OK, so if we
will stream our logs there,

00:09:03.160 --> 00:09:04.319
because usually logs--

00:09:04.319 --> 00:09:06.360
it's important data, but
it's not that important.

00:09:06.360 --> 00:09:09.200
There's actual transactional
data, and so far and so on.

00:09:09.200 --> 00:09:11.440
So the log processing
was one of the use cases

00:09:11.440 --> 00:09:13.690
where people started
to look into Kafka.

00:09:13.690 --> 00:09:16.390
Over the time, people
start realizing

00:09:16.390 --> 00:09:18.400
that, hey, actually
we can use it

00:09:18.400 --> 00:09:22.240
to solving some of the real-time
use cases, where we have data

00:09:22.240 --> 00:09:25.510
that comes in the big amounts
and the high velocity,

00:09:25.510 --> 00:09:27.700
so the Kafka helps
to absorb this.

00:09:27.700 --> 00:09:29.770
So Kafka behaves as
an actual buffer.

00:09:29.770 --> 00:09:33.190
Plus, libraries allows to
process data as it arrives.

00:09:33.190 --> 00:09:37.150
Now, use cases around Kafka
pretty much are limitless.

00:09:37.150 --> 00:09:41.620
Kafka even used like a back
bone for microservices,

00:09:41.620 --> 00:09:43.660
when you need to
establish communication

00:09:43.660 --> 00:09:47.290
between different microservices
components without actually

00:09:47.290 --> 00:09:48.221
tightly coupling them.

00:09:48.221 --> 00:09:49.720
MICHELLE CASBON:
How does it buffer?

00:09:49.720 --> 00:09:51.910
Like, can you give us
just a conceptual idea

00:09:51.910 --> 00:09:53.360
of what that really means?

00:09:53.360 --> 00:09:56.350
VIKTOR GAMOV: So Kafka itself
represents data structure

00:09:56.350 --> 00:10:01.120
that called append-only log,
or distributed append-only log,

00:10:01.120 --> 00:10:03.460
meaning that data
always will be reading

00:10:03.460 --> 00:10:06.850
to the end of the file, which
allows to very fast write

00:10:06.850 --> 00:10:10.480
capabilities, and also
can be read sequentially.

00:10:10.480 --> 00:10:13.720
So all consumers read the data
from beginning of the file,

00:10:13.720 --> 00:10:15.610
and they read this sequentially.

00:10:15.610 --> 00:10:19.700
That also allows very fast
speed in terms of consumption.

00:10:19.700 --> 00:10:22.120
This is why Kafka
looks like or sounds

00:10:22.120 --> 00:10:24.010
like a messaging
queue for many people,

00:10:24.010 --> 00:10:26.140
because in messaging
queue, your messages

00:10:26.140 --> 00:10:29.020
comes from one place and
reading from another end, right?

00:10:29.020 --> 00:10:30.940
So it's like a
first-in, first-out type

00:10:30.940 --> 00:10:31.690
of data structure.

00:10:31.690 --> 00:10:34.480
So this is why it's also
suitable for messaging

00:10:34.480 --> 00:10:35.320
use cases.

00:10:35.320 --> 00:10:39.010
Kafka provides the ways how
this data can be replicated,

00:10:39.010 --> 00:10:41.070
in case of one of the
brokers will go down,

00:10:41.070 --> 00:10:43.180
provides the way
how these topics--

00:10:43.180 --> 00:10:45.460
what we call topics, data
structure where everyone

00:10:45.460 --> 00:10:46.770
is reading and writing.

00:10:46.770 --> 00:10:48.850
And the topics allows us to--

00:10:48.850 --> 00:10:49.780
topic is partition.

00:10:49.780 --> 00:10:51.196
So this is why,
when I was talking

00:10:51.196 --> 00:10:54.310
about data structures that
lies inside the Kafka,

00:10:54.310 --> 00:10:57.890
they remind some NoSQL
type of database, where

00:10:57.890 --> 00:11:01.240
you can have a
horizontal scalability,

00:11:01.240 --> 00:11:05.170
and you can scale your topics
by just adding more brokers.

00:11:05.170 --> 00:11:08.760
And essentially it provides also
replicas in case of failures.

00:11:08.760 --> 00:11:10.510
MARK MANDEL: Are there
any kinds of limits

00:11:10.510 --> 00:11:12.710
and the type of information
that can go into Kafka,

00:11:12.710 --> 00:11:14.960
or is it kind of just like,
throw whatever you want in

00:11:14.960 --> 00:11:16.120
there to get processed?

00:11:16.120 --> 00:11:19.550
VIKTOR GAMOV: Essentially
there is no limit.

00:11:19.550 --> 00:11:21.370
But conceptually, there is.

00:11:21.370 --> 00:11:23.680
So as a streaming
platform, Kafka

00:11:23.680 --> 00:11:28.780
is designed to be handling
large number of messages.

00:11:28.780 --> 00:11:31.480
But the size of messages
should be relatively small.

00:11:31.480 --> 00:11:34.750
So this is why, if you're
trying to stream files,

00:11:34.750 --> 00:11:38.832
like 100 megabytes of
file as one message, maybe

00:11:38.832 --> 00:11:39.790
it's not very suitable.

00:11:39.790 --> 00:11:42.345
So this is why people come up
with different workarounds,

00:11:42.345 --> 00:11:44.172
so that they still
can use this use case

00:11:44.172 --> 00:11:45.630
or want to implement
this use case.

00:11:45.630 --> 00:11:49.309
So you can chunk this file and
send this in different chunks.

00:11:49.309 --> 00:11:51.100
Probably, if we're
talking about streaming,

00:11:51.100 --> 00:11:53.350
people usually talking about
streaming video or streaming

00:11:53.350 --> 00:11:53.950
audio.

00:11:53.950 --> 00:11:56.560
So Kafka is not
specifically a good case

00:11:56.560 --> 00:11:58.310
for that kind of payload.

00:11:58.310 --> 00:12:02.270
So I would say, a large number
of messages, high velocity,

00:12:02.270 --> 00:12:04.570
this is the perfect
type of payload.

00:12:04.570 --> 00:12:06.280
But inside the
message, it really

00:12:06.280 --> 00:12:08.350
doesn't matter what
you put in there.

00:12:08.350 --> 00:12:11.080
MICHELLE CASBON: So data
is, of course, key nowadays.

00:12:11.080 --> 00:12:14.150
And being able to manage it,
of course, is very important.

00:12:14.150 --> 00:12:15.760
Where does Kafka
plug in in terms

00:12:15.760 --> 00:12:19.060
of integrating the various
data, tooling, and products

00:12:19.060 --> 00:12:20.170
that exist out there?

00:12:20.170 --> 00:12:22.420
What are all the things
it integrates into?

00:12:22.420 --> 00:12:25.150
VIKTOR GAMOV: The major use
case, what I see right now--

00:12:25.150 --> 00:12:27.680
I used to work in the
professional services

00:12:27.680 --> 00:12:28.790
of Confluent.

00:12:28.790 --> 00:12:32.590
So I was talking to many
customers about their use cases

00:12:32.590 --> 00:12:34.430
and the things how
they want to use it.

00:12:34.430 --> 00:12:37.420
And specifically when we're
talking about data integration,

00:12:37.420 --> 00:12:40.790
change data capture use
case is on the high demand.

00:12:40.790 --> 00:12:44.380
So imagine you have things
like Oracle database,

00:12:44.380 --> 00:12:47.440
where you want to
extract the changes

00:12:47.440 --> 00:12:49.120
and make them real-time.

00:12:49.120 --> 00:12:51.880
So when we're talking about
usual traditional databases,

00:12:51.880 --> 00:12:54.290
it's kind of like a
solid source of data.

00:12:54.290 --> 00:12:56.900
But we want to provide a
little bit of fluidity.

00:12:56.900 --> 00:12:58.730
We need to start
moving data out.

00:12:58.730 --> 00:13:03.850
So having these connectors that
was written using Kafka Connect

00:13:03.850 --> 00:13:06.310
framework, can
extract this or listen

00:13:06.310 --> 00:13:08.710
to changes that happen
in Oracle database,

00:13:08.710 --> 00:13:10.520
and push these
changes into Kafka.

00:13:10.520 --> 00:13:14.770
So once you broke this silo
of your Oracle database,

00:13:14.770 --> 00:13:16.900
your data becomes
available for many sources.

00:13:16.900 --> 00:13:22.480
So Kafka designed to
be very heavy on reads.

00:13:22.480 --> 00:13:25.240
This is why you have this
kind of literature allusion

00:13:25.240 --> 00:13:28.570
to-- the Franz Kafka
is a famous writer.

00:13:28.570 --> 00:13:32.350
So when someone wrote this once,
and many people can read this.

00:13:32.350 --> 00:13:34.400
So same thing with Kafka.

00:13:34.400 --> 00:13:36.850
So once you extract
this data from this data

00:13:36.850 --> 00:13:39.490
silo as a traditional
relational database

00:13:39.490 --> 00:13:42.100
and start moving data
out, in this case

00:13:42.100 --> 00:13:45.730
you open up multiple different
possibilities for integration.

00:13:45.730 --> 00:13:47.410
Connectors can be two types.

00:13:47.410 --> 00:13:51.470
One can bring data in, and
another can take data out.

00:13:51.470 --> 00:13:54.520
So once you extracted
this from Oracle,

00:13:54.520 --> 00:13:58.410
you can do processing in Kafka
using Kafka Streams or KSQL,

00:13:58.410 --> 00:14:02.710
and after that offload this data
to Mongo, to Elastic, or even

00:14:02.710 --> 00:14:04.450
to any cloud storage.

00:14:04.450 --> 00:14:06.550
One of the use case
that we see right now

00:14:06.550 --> 00:14:09.080
is making Kafka as a--

00:14:09.080 --> 00:14:10.690
I don't want to
sound too marketing,

00:14:10.690 --> 00:14:12.490
but it's kind of like
a bridge to cloud.

00:14:12.490 --> 00:14:13.732
I'm sorry about that.

00:14:13.732 --> 00:14:15.190
But essentially,
one of the company

00:14:15.190 --> 00:14:19.060
that I was working, instead of
paying vendors to provide them

00:14:19.060 --> 00:14:21.035
ways how they can do
replication, and that

00:14:21.035 --> 00:14:22.450
was going to be very expensive.

00:14:22.450 --> 00:14:25.540
They use Kafka CDC
to change the capture

00:14:25.540 --> 00:14:29.110
of their on-prem database, and
replicate it to their Google

00:14:29.110 --> 00:14:31.270
Cloud Platform instances.

00:14:31.270 --> 00:14:33.760
MARK MANDEL: So if someone
needs to build a connector,

00:14:33.760 --> 00:14:36.550
or they want to build their
own custom integration,

00:14:36.550 --> 00:14:38.760
is there APIs or
client libraries

00:14:38.760 --> 00:14:39.760
for different languages?

00:14:39.760 --> 00:14:40.630
How does that work?

00:14:40.630 --> 00:14:42.046
VIKTOR GAMOV:
Usually what I tried

00:14:42.046 --> 00:14:43.930
to say, before you start
building something--

00:14:43.930 --> 00:14:45.970
and short answer to
your question, yes.

00:14:45.970 --> 00:14:47.860
But before you start
building something,

00:14:47.860 --> 00:14:50.590
you need to check if there is
a connector already available.

00:14:50.590 --> 00:14:52.390
Because the framework
itself, it's

00:14:52.390 --> 00:14:54.430
quite popular for
building connectors.

00:14:54.430 --> 00:14:58.450
There is one common place where
everyone can go and publish

00:14:58.450 --> 00:15:00.460
their connectors
called Confluent Hub.

00:15:00.460 --> 00:15:02.140
So if you go to
Confluent.io/hub,

00:15:02.140 --> 00:15:03.755
you will find all
possible connectors.

00:15:03.755 --> 00:15:04.570
MARK MANDEL: OK, cool.

00:15:04.570 --> 00:15:05.830
VIKTOR GAMOV: If you
want to build this,

00:15:05.830 --> 00:15:07.829
it will require some of
the skills, specifically

00:15:07.829 --> 00:15:08.390
Java skills.

00:15:08.390 --> 00:15:10.120
So if you want to
build a connector,

00:15:10.120 --> 00:15:11.794
there is a framework,
Kafka Connect,

00:15:11.794 --> 00:15:13.210
there's documentation
around this.

00:15:13.210 --> 00:15:15.940
There's some examples of
how to build connector.

00:15:15.940 --> 00:15:17.755
I would say it's fairly simple.

00:15:17.755 --> 00:15:19.130
Like, I built a
connector myself.

00:15:19.130 --> 00:15:22.400
So I can say that I'm not
the brightest engineer,

00:15:22.400 --> 00:15:24.050
or the smartest engineer.

00:15:24.050 --> 00:15:26.640
I can build connector, so
everyone can build connector.

00:15:26.640 --> 00:15:28.292
So API is quite straightforward.

00:15:28.292 --> 00:15:29.750
You just need to
figure out how you

00:15:29.750 --> 00:15:33.950
want to read data out of
Kafka or write data to Kafka,

00:15:33.950 --> 00:15:35.910
and that's pretty much it.

00:15:35.910 --> 00:15:38.510
However, if you don't
want to, for some reason,

00:15:38.510 --> 00:15:42.620
learn Java, or API for
your data source is not

00:15:42.620 --> 00:15:44.750
available for
Java, you can still

00:15:44.750 --> 00:15:48.080
stick to traditional
producer-consumer clients.

00:15:48.080 --> 00:15:50.300
There are plenty
native integration

00:15:50.300 --> 00:15:52.030
for any possible language.

00:15:52.030 --> 00:15:57.020
So Java, .NET,
[INAUDIBLE],, C#, C++.

00:15:57.020 --> 00:15:59.127
Just tell me when
I need to stop.

00:15:59.127 --> 00:16:00.460
MICHELLE CASBON: All the things.

00:16:00.460 --> 00:16:00.960
OK.

00:16:00.960 --> 00:16:04.500
So I understand that there is
an open source version of Kafka,

00:16:04.500 --> 00:16:06.280
and there's also an
enterprise version.

00:16:06.280 --> 00:16:08.940
So what makes them different,
and what makes them the same?

00:16:08.940 --> 00:16:12.230
VIKTOR GAMOV: So I would say
this is not a very precisely

00:16:12.230 --> 00:16:15.940
accurate statement, because
Kafka itself is open source.

00:16:15.940 --> 00:16:17.910
There's no enterprise
version of Kafka.

00:16:17.910 --> 00:16:21.830
So what we do in Confluent,
we actually building streaming

00:16:21.830 --> 00:16:26.870
platform that augments basic
things that provides by Kafka--

00:16:26.870 --> 00:16:29.810
brokers, clients, Kafka
Connect, and Kafka

00:16:29.810 --> 00:16:32.330
streams, with some
additional capabilities

00:16:32.330 --> 00:16:33.800
that enterprises need.

00:16:33.800 --> 00:16:35.810
So this is why we
build two things,

00:16:35.810 --> 00:16:39.170
we build the Confluent platform
open source and the Confluent

00:16:39.170 --> 00:16:39.890
enterprise.

00:16:39.890 --> 00:16:42.320
So for open source
platform, we include

00:16:42.320 --> 00:16:44.390
things for managing
schema for that,

00:16:44.390 --> 00:16:46.130
managing metadata
around this data

00:16:46.130 --> 00:16:48.770
called Schema
Registry, that allows

00:16:48.770 --> 00:16:51.740
you to have a
centralized place where

00:16:51.740 --> 00:16:54.320
we can store all of
these schemas of the data

00:16:54.320 --> 00:16:57.800
and provide different
versions, so one microservice

00:16:57.800 --> 00:17:01.160
can not have this data like
some built-in application.

00:17:01.160 --> 00:17:04.069
They can always request
this from Schema Registry.

00:17:04.069 --> 00:17:09.050
We provide a REST proxy, which
is, in very rare occasion,

00:17:09.050 --> 00:17:11.660
where you cannot
use native clients,

00:17:11.660 --> 00:17:13.260
you can use a REST proxy.

00:17:13.260 --> 00:17:16.450
So for example, you're
writing Kafka client in BASH,

00:17:16.450 --> 00:17:18.780
and you're using cURL
to interact with Kafka.

00:17:18.780 --> 00:17:20.579
So you can use a REST
proxy to do that.

00:17:20.579 --> 00:17:22.730
And the third one, I
guess, very cool thing,

00:17:22.730 --> 00:17:27.020
is the KSQL, which is
stream processing tool that

00:17:27.020 --> 00:17:29.960
allows you to use SQL to
write your stream processing

00:17:29.960 --> 00:17:30.502
applications.

00:17:30.502 --> 00:17:32.918
MARK MANDEL: How do you write
SQL to do stream processing?

00:17:32.918 --> 00:17:33.750
How does that work?

00:17:33.750 --> 00:17:35.060
VIKTOR GAMOV: So we
were thinking that, OK,

00:17:35.060 --> 00:17:37.100
how we can liberate
the stream processing

00:17:37.100 --> 00:17:39.500
and open up to a wider audience.

00:17:39.500 --> 00:17:43.730
And we figured out that the
most used language, or most

00:17:43.730 --> 00:17:46.880
used technology, in
data world is SQL.

00:17:46.880 --> 00:17:51.620
So we said, OK, let's see
how we can use a SQL language

00:17:51.620 --> 00:17:53.480
as a DSL for stream processing.

00:17:53.480 --> 00:17:55.610
So essentially, we took
some of the concept

00:17:55.610 --> 00:17:58.400
that people already
know-- do your select,

00:17:58.400 --> 00:18:00.830
and you filtering by
in-particular fields,

00:18:00.830 --> 00:18:03.700
and we build a tool called KSQL.

00:18:03.700 --> 00:18:07.190
So you have an infinite stream
of credit card transactions,

00:18:07.190 --> 00:18:10.190
but you're only
interested in data

00:18:10.190 --> 00:18:14.510
that happened with one account
within five-second interval,

00:18:14.510 --> 00:18:16.370
so you have multiple swipes.

00:18:16.370 --> 00:18:18.590
So you can potentially
say, OK, so maybe it's

00:18:18.590 --> 00:18:21.020
a fraudulent account within
five-second interval.

00:18:21.020 --> 00:18:25.280
So KSQL allows you to
write simple SQL statement

00:18:25.280 --> 00:18:27.830
on infinite stream of
data that will produce

00:18:27.830 --> 00:18:30.211
another stream that will
include all the data that you're

00:18:30.211 --> 00:18:30.710
interested.

00:18:30.710 --> 00:18:34.340
So for example, from the stream
of credit card transactions,

00:18:34.340 --> 00:18:36.380
we can produce
stream that will have

00:18:36.380 --> 00:18:40.160
potential fraudulent
transactions, that will include

00:18:40.160 --> 00:18:43.430
all messages where credit
card number ID will

00:18:43.430 --> 00:18:45.950
be swiped three
times or five times

00:18:45.950 --> 00:18:47.360
within five-minute interval.

00:18:47.360 --> 00:18:50.270
So it is a powerful
thing, because it includes

00:18:50.270 --> 00:18:53.360
what we call stateful stream
processing, because in order

00:18:53.360 --> 00:18:57.350
to do a count, how many times
you swiped a credit card,

00:18:57.350 --> 00:19:00.320
you need to have a result from
the previous message, right?

00:19:00.320 --> 00:19:02.000
So you need to have
it stored somewhere.

00:19:02.000 --> 00:19:02.910
You need to have a state.

00:19:02.910 --> 00:19:04.534
And also, you need
to have a state that

00:19:04.534 --> 00:19:06.530
will be applicable to a
particular time window.

00:19:06.530 --> 00:19:08.450
Window is essentially
a concept that

00:19:08.450 --> 00:19:11.070
allows you to build
finite data chunks

00:19:11.070 --> 00:19:12.410
on infinite stream of data.

00:19:12.410 --> 00:19:16.130
And everything was done
thanks to Kafka Stream.

00:19:16.130 --> 00:19:19.910
So KSQL is built onto Kafka
Streams, which is Java library.

00:19:19.910 --> 00:19:22.010
But as a developer,
you don't need

00:19:22.010 --> 00:19:24.440
to learn Java or
any other language.

00:19:24.440 --> 00:19:27.010
Just use SQL to write
your application.

00:19:27.010 --> 00:19:29.570
I am not sure if I
answered this fully,

00:19:29.570 --> 00:19:31.640
but essentially, there's
a couple extensions

00:19:31.640 --> 00:19:33.560
that you need to know,
because in standard SQL

00:19:33.560 --> 00:19:35.250
there is no stream concept.

00:19:35.250 --> 00:19:36.757
There's no window concept.

00:19:36.757 --> 00:19:38.840
However, if you want to
[INAUDIBLE],, for example,

00:19:38.840 --> 00:19:42.050
you have data that
comes with the stream,

00:19:42.050 --> 00:19:45.080
and you have some data that you
extracted from your database,

00:19:45.080 --> 00:19:46.640
and this data inside the Kafka.

00:19:46.640 --> 00:19:49.070
You can join those
two streams in order

00:19:49.070 --> 00:19:50.810
to produce enriched stream.

00:19:50.810 --> 00:19:53.990
You have a stream that only
includes credit card number

00:19:53.990 --> 00:19:57.320
information, and have another
stream that has credit card

00:19:57.320 --> 00:20:01.310
number and all fields
that is required

00:20:01.310 --> 00:20:04.310
to know who the owner
of the credit card, what

00:20:04.310 --> 00:20:06.260
is the balance, and
things like that.

00:20:06.260 --> 00:20:08.090
So you're joining
two streams in order

00:20:08.090 --> 00:20:11.350
to produce third stream, which
would be augmented stream.

00:20:11.350 --> 00:20:14.930
And to quickly return to the
question about enterprise

00:20:14.930 --> 00:20:17.260
platform-- so enterprise
platform includes

00:20:17.260 --> 00:20:19.960
some of the monitoring
tools, some advanced tools

00:20:19.960 --> 00:20:23.050
for data replication,
and essentially it's

00:20:23.050 --> 00:20:25.690
also built on top of
open source Kafka,

00:20:25.690 --> 00:20:28.210
on top of open source
platform, and provides

00:20:28.210 --> 00:20:30.610
some of the enterprise
tools like for monitoring,

00:20:30.610 --> 00:20:32.380
for example, control
center, and some

00:20:32.380 --> 00:20:34.870
of the connectors for
enterprise systems.

00:20:34.870 --> 00:20:37.670
But Kafka itself, it's
fully open source.

00:20:37.670 --> 00:20:41.080
And there's no difference
between enterprise platform

00:20:41.080 --> 00:20:42.082
and open source Kafka.

00:20:42.082 --> 00:20:43.040
MICHELLE CASBON: Great.

00:20:43.040 --> 00:20:45.880
So what is the relationship
between Confluent and the Kafka

00:20:45.880 --> 00:20:46.720
project?

00:20:46.720 --> 00:20:49.540
VIKTOR GAMOV: So
the Confluent was

00:20:49.540 --> 00:20:53.590
founded by three people
that came from LinkedIn,

00:20:53.590 --> 00:20:56.860
and those people were
active contributors

00:20:56.860 --> 00:20:58.390
to Apache Kafka project.

00:20:58.390 --> 00:21:02.050
So Jay Kreps was one of
the masterminds behind some

00:21:02.050 --> 00:21:04.620
of the distributed
systems in LinkedIn,

00:21:04.620 --> 00:21:06.010
and the Kafka was included.

00:21:06.010 --> 00:21:09.140
And one of the most
popular project is Kafka.

00:21:09.140 --> 00:21:12.190
So he helped to bring this to
Apache Software Foundation.

00:21:12.190 --> 00:21:18.070
And after that, when the project
was very popular in Apache,

00:21:18.070 --> 00:21:21.370
they decided to start a company
to provide streaming platform

00:21:21.370 --> 00:21:23.800
on top of Apache
Kafka that will have

00:21:23.800 --> 00:21:26.140
all features of
open source thing,

00:21:26.140 --> 00:21:28.930
but also will have
advanced features

00:21:28.930 --> 00:21:32.360
and tools that modern
enterprise require,

00:21:32.360 --> 00:21:35.230
for example, like for data
governance and schema registry,

00:21:35.230 --> 00:21:39.070
or for providing simplicity
of stream processing,

00:21:39.070 --> 00:21:41.050
providing KSQL,
so far and so on.

00:21:41.050 --> 00:21:43.240
So right now, I don't
remember exact number,

00:21:43.240 --> 00:21:45.730
but Confluent is the
largest contributor

00:21:45.730 --> 00:21:47.330
to open source Apache Kafka.

00:21:47.330 --> 00:21:51.040
So we have engineers who
work on pushing the bits

00:21:51.040 --> 00:21:53.210
and bytes to Apache Kafka.

00:21:53.210 --> 00:21:56.140
MARK MANDEL: So this is the
Google Cloud Platform Podcast.

00:21:56.140 --> 00:21:58.360
What's the relationship
between Confluent and Google

00:21:58.360 --> 00:21:59.410
Cloud at the moment?

00:21:59.410 --> 00:22:01.850
VIKTOR GAMOV: I would
say, maybe two years ago,

00:22:01.850 --> 00:22:03.580
we would start getting
a lot of requests

00:22:03.580 --> 00:22:06.400
from customers saying that, hey,
so it would be great if there

00:22:06.400 --> 00:22:08.450
would be a managed to Kafka--

00:22:08.450 --> 00:22:09.550
I'm sorry.

00:22:09.550 --> 00:22:11.860
I'm starting from a
little bit of history

00:22:11.860 --> 00:22:13.480
to put some of the
context in place.

00:22:13.480 --> 00:22:15.520
So the customer was
saying, yeah, it

00:22:15.520 --> 00:22:17.500
would be great to
have managed Kafka,

00:22:17.500 --> 00:22:21.520
because we as enterprises
want to focus on getting fast

00:22:21.520 --> 00:22:25.120
into cloud, and getting fast
into solving our business

00:22:25.120 --> 00:22:27.260
problems rather than
building infrastructure.

00:22:27.260 --> 00:22:30.430
So we start building what we
call the Confluent Cloud, which

00:22:30.430 --> 00:22:34.210
is essentially a fully
managed solution for running

00:22:34.210 --> 00:22:37.130
not only Apache Kafka, but
full Confluent platform.

00:22:37.130 --> 00:22:40.510
So one of the things
that we had in mind

00:22:40.510 --> 00:22:43.710
is that we don't want
people manage anything.

00:22:43.710 --> 00:22:47.350
You don't need to give us
your account information

00:22:47.350 --> 00:22:49.940
if you want ours to
run on your hardware.

00:22:49.940 --> 00:22:53.440
So we can say, yes, we can
run Kafka and the Confluent

00:22:53.440 --> 00:22:58.330
platform in any public cloud
provider, so pick and choose.

00:22:58.330 --> 00:23:00.370
So we released
everything in AWS,

00:23:00.370 --> 00:23:02.910
and last year was huge
success of launch,

00:23:02.910 --> 00:23:06.040
a long-awaited launch of
Confluent Cloud in the Google

00:23:06.040 --> 00:23:07.300
Cloud Platform.

00:23:07.300 --> 00:23:09.430
Some of the things
that we initially

00:23:09.430 --> 00:23:11.680
built for this
platform was we're

00:23:11.680 --> 00:23:14.830
architected to support
running into multiple cloud

00:23:14.830 --> 00:23:16.220
environments, for example.

00:23:16.220 --> 00:23:18.220
So essentially, we run
everything in Kubernetes.

00:23:18.220 --> 00:23:20.620
So Kubernetes is our
weapon of choice.

00:23:20.620 --> 00:23:23.444
MICHELLE CASBON: Mark's doing
a happy dance about Kubernetes.

00:23:23.444 --> 00:23:25.360
VIKTOR GAMOV: Keep this,
the Kubernetes thing,

00:23:25.360 --> 00:23:26.500
on the back burner.

00:23:26.500 --> 00:23:28.270
We have some news
around this, as well.

00:23:28.270 --> 00:23:31.120
When we move to
another cloud from AWS,

00:23:31.120 --> 00:23:33.400
we actually had to
rearchitect other things,

00:23:33.400 --> 00:23:36.610
because lot of things
were easy to do in AWS,

00:23:36.610 --> 00:23:38.907
some of the things were
easy to do in Google Cloud.

00:23:38.907 --> 00:23:40.990
For example, like to spin
up the Kubernetes class,

00:23:40.990 --> 00:23:43.900
that is much easier in
the Google Cloud Platform.

00:23:43.900 --> 00:23:46.420
Or to configure
some of the things--

00:23:46.420 --> 00:23:48.140
because simply it
was more popular,

00:23:48.140 --> 00:23:51.280
AWS has some certain API
to do load balancing.

00:23:51.280 --> 00:23:52.730
But right now, it's a parity.

00:23:52.730 --> 00:23:54.602
So when you go to
Confluent Cloud,

00:23:54.602 --> 00:23:56.810
and you want to spin up your
Kafka, you can say, hey,

00:23:56.810 --> 00:23:58.810
I want to run this
in my Google Cloud.

00:23:58.810 --> 00:24:01.720
You can just select the
region, and after that you're

00:24:01.720 --> 00:24:02.320
good to go.

00:24:02.320 --> 00:24:04.810
So you don't need to think
where this is running.

00:24:04.810 --> 00:24:07.360
You just have a connection
stream to Kafka,

00:24:07.360 --> 00:24:08.941
and that's pretty much it.

00:24:08.941 --> 00:24:11.065
Another thing that I
mentioned about the Kubernetes

00:24:11.065 --> 00:24:15.460
is that we internally
gain a lot of experience

00:24:15.460 --> 00:24:17.340
by running this at scale.

00:24:17.340 --> 00:24:20.110
And some of the customers, like
more conservative customers,

00:24:20.110 --> 00:24:22.300
are saying, hey yeah,
the cloud is cool,

00:24:22.300 --> 00:24:26.840
but how I would have these same
things that cloud provides,

00:24:26.840 --> 00:24:28.030
but also internally.

00:24:28.030 --> 00:24:29.590
So we have internal tasks.

00:24:29.590 --> 00:24:31.090
For example, we're
running something

00:24:31.090 --> 00:24:32.740
like open source
Kubernetes, or when

00:24:32.740 --> 00:24:36.370
it will be available publicly,
we can run a GCP on-prem.

00:24:36.370 --> 00:24:38.810
How we can run the Kafka
same way as you do.

00:24:38.810 --> 00:24:43.270
So that's why we also invested
to product called the Confluent

00:24:43.270 --> 00:24:47.350
Operator that allows you to
just deal with your requirements

00:24:47.350 --> 00:24:47.890
about Kafka.

00:24:47.890 --> 00:24:51.760
You don't need to think about
what's the StatefulSet, what's

00:24:51.760 --> 00:24:54.850
your service, what's the
headless service, how

00:24:54.850 --> 00:24:57.940
to expose your Kafka
to outside world,

00:24:57.940 --> 00:25:00.520
how to build load balance
or [INAUDIBLE] operator

00:25:00.520 --> 00:25:04.540
inside Kubernetes, will do same
thing as it does in our cloud,

00:25:04.540 --> 00:25:08.050
but it allows the customers to
run this in their data center

00:25:08.050 --> 00:25:09.094
on-prem or in cloud.

00:25:09.094 --> 00:25:11.260
MARK MANDEL: I literally
just looked at the website.

00:25:11.260 --> 00:25:13.090
It says here, "available soon."

00:25:13.090 --> 00:25:14.230
Is that all you can say?

00:25:14.230 --> 00:25:15.021
VIKTOR GAMOV: Yeah.

00:25:15.021 --> 00:25:17.340
It will be available
hopefully the beginning

00:25:17.340 --> 00:25:18.750
of the year, 2019.

00:25:18.750 --> 00:25:21.640
So right now, we
onboard a few customers,

00:25:21.640 --> 00:25:24.330
we run very rigorous
early-access program.

00:25:24.330 --> 00:25:26.940
We're running some
of the edge cases

00:25:26.940 --> 00:25:30.840
to make sure that product
would meet all expectations.

00:25:30.840 --> 00:25:33.960
I already started
playing with this myself.

00:25:33.960 --> 00:25:35.970
I also presented this
in a few conferences.

00:25:35.970 --> 00:25:37.470
It looks pretty awesome.

00:25:37.470 --> 00:25:39.930
In order to provide
the best experience,

00:25:39.930 --> 00:25:42.370
we just need to have
a little more testing

00:25:42.370 --> 00:25:44.930
with some of our partners
and some of the customers.

00:25:44.930 --> 00:25:45.680
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:25:45.680 --> 00:25:45.890
VIKTOR GAMOV: Yeah.

00:25:45.890 --> 00:25:48.150
Personally, again, no
one paid me for this,

00:25:48.150 --> 00:25:50.220
but I personally use
GCP for all my tests,

00:25:50.220 --> 00:25:52.770
because again, right now
it's the best experience,

00:25:52.770 --> 00:25:56.992
if you want to have Kubernetes
in public cloud, is GCP.

00:25:56.992 --> 00:25:57.950
MARK MANDEL: Thank you.

00:25:57.950 --> 00:26:00.355
VIKTOR GAMOV: So love
it, use it personally.

00:26:00.355 --> 00:26:01.230
MARK MANDEL: Awesome.

00:26:01.230 --> 00:26:03.313
It sounds like you're
building some amazing stuff.

00:26:03.313 --> 00:26:06.061
What can we expect from Kafka
and Confluent in the future?

00:26:06.061 --> 00:26:07.560
I mean, you mentioned
the operators,

00:26:07.560 --> 00:26:09.720
but is there any other neat
stuff coming in the pipeline

00:26:09.720 --> 00:26:11.030
that people might
want to know about?

00:26:11.030 --> 00:26:13.029
VIKTOR GAMOV: Hoo, this
is a very good question.

00:26:13.029 --> 00:26:15.570
So we still continue
to pushing boundaries

00:26:15.570 --> 00:26:18.540
of what the KSQL can do,
some advanced semantics

00:26:18.540 --> 00:26:20.400
supporting some advanced schema.

00:26:20.400 --> 00:26:24.420
So right now, KSQL allows you
to, without any interference,

00:26:24.420 --> 00:26:28.680
KSQL allows to deal with JSON,
Avro, and [INAUDIBLE],, some

00:26:28.680 --> 00:26:30.090
of the comma-separated data.

00:26:30.090 --> 00:26:33.400
We're thinking to have more
schemas coming, for example,

00:26:33.400 --> 00:26:36.420
gRPC and the Google Protocol
Buffers, that's very

00:26:36.420 --> 00:26:39.030
popular formatting services.

00:26:39.030 --> 00:26:41.400
We're thinking to support
this, and we have a lot

00:26:41.400 --> 00:26:42.780
of requests from the customers.

00:26:42.780 --> 00:26:45.390
In terms of cloud,
we keep pushing

00:26:45.390 --> 00:26:48.690
some of the observability
elements in the Confluent

00:26:48.690 --> 00:26:52.890
Cloud, providing metrics you
can see, how fast this data is

00:26:52.890 --> 00:26:55.710
going through, what's
the all possible metrics

00:26:55.710 --> 00:26:58.330
that your application
can depend on?

00:26:58.330 --> 00:27:02.490
We planning to have full
managed Confluent platform,

00:27:02.490 --> 00:27:06.000
all bits and bytes of Confluent
platform will be in our cloud.

00:27:06.000 --> 00:27:07.500
And most importantly,
we're planning

00:27:07.500 --> 00:27:10.240
to have free tier for
our Kafka As a Service.

00:27:10.240 --> 00:27:13.050
So when you want to
start learning things,

00:27:13.050 --> 00:27:15.910
you don't need to have data
center to spin up this.

00:27:15.910 --> 00:27:18.120
You just go there,
run your Kafka,

00:27:18.120 --> 00:27:19.530
grab some of the libraries.

00:27:19.530 --> 00:27:22.320
My team is responsible
for helping developers

00:27:22.320 --> 00:27:26.610
to have enough examples, to have
enough documentation in order

00:27:26.610 --> 00:27:28.190
to start with Kafka.

00:27:28.190 --> 00:27:29.880
And we're actually
pretty excited when

00:27:29.880 --> 00:27:32.430
we will release free
version of Cloud,

00:27:32.430 --> 00:27:36.220
so we can talk more about
libraries, languages.

00:27:36.220 --> 00:27:39.240
I'm pretty excited to talk
about Go Client myself.

00:27:39.240 --> 00:27:40.950
I start Go Development recently.

00:27:40.950 --> 00:27:41.760
MARK MANDEL: Yay!

00:27:41.760 --> 00:27:43.009
MICHELLE CASBON: That's great.

00:27:43.009 --> 00:27:45.570
If I wanted to contribute to
actually building out Kafka,

00:27:45.570 --> 00:27:47.700
since it's open source,
how can I do that?

00:27:47.700 --> 00:27:50.970
VIKTOR GAMOV: So if you want
to contribute to Apache Kafka,

00:27:50.970 --> 00:27:55.170
it's great that the products
like Kafka and Hadoop,

00:27:55.170 --> 00:27:57.740
they're placed in a
well-established software

00:27:57.740 --> 00:28:01.870
foundation that has certain
rules about how you do things.

00:28:01.870 --> 00:28:05.820
And there's actually very clear
ways how you can contribute.

00:28:05.820 --> 00:28:09.200
You start by finding
some issues in Jira.

00:28:09.200 --> 00:28:10.290
Jira is public.

00:28:10.290 --> 00:28:13.230
You can go, there's
Kafka Jira in Apache,

00:28:13.230 --> 00:28:15.330
you start looking
for some issues.

00:28:15.330 --> 00:28:17.010
You can go there,
comment saying, hey,

00:28:17.010 --> 00:28:19.260
I'm interested to
fix this problem.

00:28:19.260 --> 00:28:22.920
So you start working on the
thing, either providing patch,

00:28:22.920 --> 00:28:25.740
or you're creating pull
requests, because development

00:28:25.740 --> 00:28:27.670
also happen in GitHub.

00:28:27.670 --> 00:28:30.520
So there is Apache
mirror of repository,

00:28:30.520 --> 00:28:33.689
so you can also do
development in GitHub.

00:28:33.689 --> 00:28:34.980
So you're providing your patch.

00:28:34.980 --> 00:28:38.370
You discuss through code review,
you discuss with contributors.

00:28:38.370 --> 00:28:41.100
One of the contributors
or committers,

00:28:41.100 --> 00:28:45.150
they can be your guide, so they
can explain what you're doing,

00:28:45.150 --> 00:28:46.980
if everything is
OK, everyone is OK,

00:28:46.980 --> 00:28:49.080
so your pull request
will be merged.

00:28:49.080 --> 00:28:51.960
So over time you will
gain more and more power

00:28:51.960 --> 00:28:54.270
as a [? contributor ?] that
you can become a committer,

00:28:54.270 --> 00:28:56.790
and you can start to
send more patches.

00:28:56.790 --> 00:28:59.490
And over the time, you
can even start your own,

00:28:59.490 --> 00:29:02.040
if you want to introduce
some new feature,

00:29:02.040 --> 00:29:04.530
introduce some new
piece of functionality,

00:29:04.530 --> 00:29:07.800
you can start your own, called
a Kafka Improvement Proposal,

00:29:07.800 --> 00:29:10.590
or KIP, where you can
discuss some of the design

00:29:10.590 --> 00:29:12.470
decision or some new features.

00:29:12.470 --> 00:29:14.260
And a cool thing
about, usually people

00:29:14.260 --> 00:29:17.160
are also asking me, if I
want to learn some internals,

00:29:17.160 --> 00:29:20.040
if I want to learn how
different functionality works,

00:29:20.040 --> 00:29:21.390
how I can learn this?

00:29:21.390 --> 00:29:23.460
And you can go and
read certain KIPs,

00:29:23.460 --> 00:29:25.710
because KIP is
essentially your blueprint

00:29:25.710 --> 00:29:28.800
for certain functionality,
like a very cool way how

00:29:28.800 --> 00:29:30.150
you can learn from the source.

00:29:30.150 --> 00:29:32.760
And the thought
process, there's also

00:29:32.760 --> 00:29:36.191
some of the design decisions
that were rejected also

00:29:36.191 --> 00:29:36.690
mentioned.

00:29:36.690 --> 00:29:37.900
It's also pretty cool.

00:29:37.900 --> 00:29:40.760
If you want to contribute to
Confluent open source platform,

00:29:40.760 --> 00:29:43.350
or like KSQL, it's less strict.

00:29:43.350 --> 00:29:47.850
So essentially, go there, for
KSQL, send your pull request,

00:29:47.850 --> 00:29:50.220
you will discuss with
engineers, and you just

00:29:50.220 --> 00:29:53.299
need to sign contributor
agreement, and pretty much it.

00:29:53.299 --> 00:29:54.090
MARK MANDEL: Sweet.

00:29:54.090 --> 00:29:56.435
If people want to learn more
about Kafka or Confluent,

00:29:56.435 --> 00:29:57.310
where should they go?

00:29:57.310 --> 00:29:58.893
VIKTOR GAMOV: So I
would say they need

00:29:58.893 --> 00:30:00.360
to go just the Confluent.io.

00:30:00.360 --> 00:30:02.880
And we're working
right now, probably

00:30:02.880 --> 00:30:05.094
you should expect this
in 2019 somewhere,

00:30:05.094 --> 00:30:06.510
we're working on
some landing page

00:30:06.510 --> 00:30:10.140
for developers, the
architects, and administrators.

00:30:10.140 --> 00:30:13.410
But right now, Confluent.io,
it's a great landing page.

00:30:13.410 --> 00:30:16.280
I would highly recommend
to subscribe to our blog.

00:30:16.280 --> 00:30:20.570
Trying to share not only our
success stories, but also some

00:30:20.570 --> 00:30:22.670
of the customers, they
contribute to our blog.

00:30:22.670 --> 00:30:24.980
Some of the individual
contributors,

00:30:24.980 --> 00:30:26.510
they also write for us.

00:30:26.510 --> 00:30:30.410
So our blog, you can see
there's like very good source

00:30:30.410 --> 00:30:33.920
of all sorts of information,
starting from some internals

00:30:33.920 --> 00:30:35.220
about Kafka.

00:30:35.220 --> 00:30:38.000
So June Rao is one of the
co-founders of Confluent,

00:30:38.000 --> 00:30:40.400
and one of the core
committers of Kafka.

00:30:40.400 --> 00:30:42.589
He has a very famous
blog about how

00:30:42.589 --> 00:30:43.880
to choose number of partitions.

00:30:43.880 --> 00:30:46.640
And even Google, when
you do a Google search,

00:30:46.640 --> 00:30:48.800
you will find this
blog faster than you

00:30:48.800 --> 00:30:50.420
will find actual documentation.

00:30:50.420 --> 00:30:54.020
So it's a very popular and
highly-regarded blog post.

00:30:54.020 --> 00:30:57.620
We have interesting use
cases from some customers.

00:30:57.620 --> 00:31:00.680
One of the popular blog posts
is from "New York Times,"

00:31:00.680 --> 00:31:04.370
and how they use Kafka to
store all the data, or all

00:31:04.370 --> 00:31:07.040
the published materials that
they have on their website,

00:31:07.040 --> 00:31:09.700
they stored inside a Kafka
topic, so also pretty cool.

00:31:09.700 --> 00:31:12.210
And you looking into Google,
some of the use cases,

00:31:12.210 --> 00:31:14.840
and most likely you will end
up on the Confluent blog.

00:31:14.840 --> 00:31:16.670
I would say, the
best place to start.

00:31:16.670 --> 00:31:17.150
MICHELLE CASBON: Great.

