MARK MANDEL: There we go.

00:33:04.700 --> 00:33:06.660
Let's get stuck into our
question of the week.

00:33:06.660 --> 00:33:09.920
So I do hear that Cloud
Next 2019 is coming up,

00:33:09.920 --> 00:33:12.110
and I would really like
to submit something

00:33:12.110 --> 00:33:13.040
so that I can present.

00:33:13.040 --> 00:33:14.420
So is there a call for papers?

00:33:14.420 --> 00:33:15.080
Can I submit?

00:33:15.080 --> 00:33:15.789
How do I do this?

00:33:15.789 --> 00:33:16.871
MICHELLE CASBON: There is.

00:33:16.871 --> 00:33:18.020
There's a whole website.

00:33:18.020 --> 00:33:19.760
You can find the link
in the show notes.

00:33:19.760 --> 00:33:22.200
There's a ton of
information on this page.

00:33:22.200 --> 00:33:24.770
So this will be
my first Next CFP.

00:33:24.770 --> 00:33:26.870
And so I, just this
weekend, was looking

00:33:26.870 --> 00:33:29.090
over all of the guidelines,
tips and tricks.

00:33:29.090 --> 00:33:32.420
So I will share some of the,
I think, most useful advice.

00:33:32.420 --> 00:33:34.850
So the first thing you
want to keep in mind

00:33:34.850 --> 00:33:36.920
is that it's not
really about just

00:33:36.920 --> 00:33:39.470
describing some cool
project that you've done.

00:33:39.470 --> 00:33:42.080
You want to focus on what
people can get out of your talk.

00:33:42.080 --> 00:33:44.750
So what are the main things that
they will learn after they've

00:33:44.750 --> 00:33:45.859
visited your session?

00:33:45.859 --> 00:33:47.150
This is how you draw people in.

00:33:47.150 --> 00:33:48.911
This is how you
make it compelling.

00:33:48.911 --> 00:33:50.660
And you want to focus
on either a beginner

00:33:50.660 --> 00:33:52.220
level, intermediate,
or advanced,

00:33:52.220 --> 00:33:54.590
but be very clear about
what you'll be covering

00:33:54.590 --> 00:33:56.300
and how many prerequisites
are involved.

00:33:56.300 --> 00:33:58.760
So the word counts
are actually pretty

00:33:58.760 --> 00:34:02.060
short on this, which I
appreciate, especially

00:34:02.060 --> 00:34:02.990
as someone who--

00:34:02.990 --> 00:34:04.281
MARK MANDEL: It drives me nuts.

00:34:04.281 --> 00:34:06.000
I can't stop but
write long things.

00:34:06.000 --> 00:34:08.810
MICHELLE CASBON: Yes, you are
pretty bad about following

00:34:08.810 --> 00:34:10.409
the word count limits.

00:34:10.409 --> 00:34:12.927
But this one, it's like 250
words for your abstract.

00:34:12.927 --> 00:34:13.760
MARK MANDEL: Oh, my.

00:34:13.760 --> 00:34:15.218
MICHELLE CASBON:
It's really short.

00:34:15.218 --> 00:34:17.210
But that makes it a
lot easier and harder

00:34:17.210 --> 00:34:18.876
to submit your abstract.

00:34:18.876 --> 00:34:20.000
MARK MANDEL: I'm terrified.

00:34:20.000 --> 00:34:22.041
MICHELLE CASBON: So you
want to be very efficient

00:34:22.041 --> 00:34:24.830
with your words, like high
impact, heavy hitting, what

00:34:24.830 --> 00:34:25.896
are you going to teach?

00:34:25.896 --> 00:34:27.020
What are the big takeaways?

00:34:27.020 --> 00:34:28.519
MARK MANDEL: When
I write abstracts,

00:34:28.519 --> 00:34:30.080
I have a formula
that I like to use

00:34:30.080 --> 00:34:31.746
that I think has
worked reasonably well,

00:34:31.746 --> 00:34:33.570
which is, what is the problem?

00:34:33.570 --> 00:34:34.460
What is the solution?

00:34:34.460 --> 00:34:36.610
And then, in this talk,
what are you going to show?

00:34:36.610 --> 00:34:38.280
And I find that works
reasonably well.

00:34:38.280 --> 00:34:40.819
So like, in some planets,
you can't pet dogs.

00:34:40.819 --> 00:34:41.360
[DOG BARKING]

00:34:41.360 --> 00:34:43.190
So today we're going to talk
about how we can bring dogs

00:34:43.190 --> 00:34:44.076
to your planet.

00:34:44.076 --> 00:34:46.409
In this talk, we'll talk about
bringing dogs to planets,

00:34:46.409 --> 00:34:47.825
and the problems
you may run into.

00:34:47.825 --> 00:34:50.170
Like, that is literally
what my abstracts look like.

00:34:50.170 --> 00:34:50.690
MICHELLE CASBON: Nice.

00:34:50.690 --> 00:34:52.100
So you're hoping to
draw on people who

00:34:52.100 --> 00:34:53.270
live on planets with no dogs.

00:34:53.270 --> 00:34:54.130
MARK MANDEL: Exactly.

00:34:54.130 --> 00:34:55.170
MICHELLE CASBON: And it's
there in the abstract.

00:34:55.170 --> 00:34:55.880
It's very clear.

00:34:55.880 --> 00:34:58.340
They know exactly that they
belong in that session.

00:34:58.340 --> 00:34:59.360
MARK MANDEL: And they
know exactly what

00:34:59.360 --> 00:35:00.420
they're going to get out of it.

00:35:00.420 --> 00:35:02.990
I find that-- that's a formula
I've used a lot over the years.

00:35:02.990 --> 00:35:04.330
MICHELLE CASBON: Yeah,
that's a good model.

00:35:04.330 --> 00:35:06.560
And if you want to see
examples of abstracts

00:35:06.560 --> 00:35:08.900
that have been accepted,
there's a whole PDF

00:35:08.900 --> 00:35:13.700
with about a dozen examples
for you to use as a guide.

00:35:13.700 --> 00:35:18.210
And so whatever subject area
you think is most interesting--

00:35:18.210 --> 00:35:20.020
preferably it has a
Google product in it.

00:35:20.020 --> 00:35:21.159
It is a Google conference.

00:35:21.159 --> 00:35:21.950
MARK MANDEL: Sweet.

00:35:21.950 --> 00:35:22.866
MICHELLE CASBON: Yeah.

00:35:22.866 --> 00:35:24.810
So you have about a
month to submit this.

00:35:24.810 --> 00:35:26.310
Deadline is December 7.

00:35:26.310 --> 00:35:30.120
So get your ideas together, and
make sure you get it in time.

00:35:30.120 --> 00:35:32.100
